  "solution": {
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "square",
        "kind": "concept",
        "title": "Square both sides",
        "caption": "Squaring can create **extra** solutions, so we will check them at the end.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "tex": "x + 5 = \\htmlClass{hlbox}{(x - 7)^2}",
            "relation": "squaring"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "expand",
        "kind": "simple",
        "title": "Expand and rearrange",
        "caption": "Careful: $(x - 7)^2 = x^2 - 14x + 49$, not $x^2 + 49$.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "tex": "x + 5 = \\htmlClass{hlbox}{x^2 - 14x + 49}",
            "relation": "equivalent"
          },
          {
            "tex": "0 = x^2 - 15x + 44",
            "relation": "equivalent"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "factor",
        "kind": "simple",
        "title": "Factor",
        "caption": "Product $44$, sum $-15$: use $-4$ and $-11$.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "tex": "0 = (x - 4)(x - 11)",
            "relation": "equivalent"
          },
          {
            "tex": "x = 4\\ \\text{or}\\ x = 11",
            "relation": "candidates",
            "values": [
              4,
              11
            ],
            "label": "Candidates · not answers yet"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "check_valid",
        "kind": "concept",
        "title": "Test x = 11",
        "caption": "Substitute into the **original** equation.",
        "check": {
          "value": 11,
          "valid": true,
          "lines": [
            {
              "tex": "\\sqrt{11 + 5} \\stackrel{?}{=} 11 - 7",
              "lhs": "\\sqrt{11 + 5}",
              "rhs": "11 - 7"
            },
            {
              "tex": "4 = 4",
              "lhs": "4",
              "rhs": "4",
              "relation": "="
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "check_extraneous",
        "kind": "concept",
        "title": "Test x = 4",
        "caption": "Substitute into the **original** equation.",
        "check": {
          "value": 4,
          "valid": false,
          "lines": [
            {
              "tex": "\\sqrt{4 + 5} \\stackrel{?}{=} 4 - 7",
              "lhs": "\\sqrt{4 + 5}",
              "rhs": "4 - 7"
            },
            {
              "tex": "3 \\neq \\htmlClass{hlbad}{-3}",
              "lhs": "3",
              "rhs": "-3",
              "relation": "\\neq"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "why",
        "kind": "concept",
        "title": "Why did 4 appear?",
        "caption": "A principal square root is never negative, so it can't equal $-3$.",
        "caption2": "Squaring hid the sign, so $4$ slipped in.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "tex": "3^2 = (-3)^2 = 9",
            "relation": "numeric"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "keyIdea": "Squaring both sides can create **extraneous solutions**. Always check every candidate in the **original** equation.",
    "trap": null,
    "summary": "Square both sides: x + 5 = (x - 7)^2, so x^2 - 15x + 44 = 0 and x = 4 or x = 11. In the original equation, x = 11 gives sqrt(16) = 4 = 11 - 7, so it works. x = 4 gives sqrt(9) = 3 but 4 - 7 = -3; a principal square root is never negative, so 4 is extraneous. Squaring hid the sign (3^2 = (-3)^2). Answer: x = 11 only."
  },
  "meta": {
    "title_hook": "Squaring Both Sides Can Fool You",
    "description_lead": "Solve √(x + 5) = x − 7. Squaring gives two candidates, but only one of them is a real solution. Can you spot the fake?"
  },
